# THE EXPENSIVE CHECKS -- pipe-separated, one row per check. Columns:
#
#   check | command | minutes | due_after | artefact |
#   last_run | last_commit | last_result | note | on_surprise
#
# ``tools/cadence.py`` reads this file. ``./check.ps1`` prints what is due at
# the end of every run, and the handoff skill will not close a session that
# leaves a due check unnamed in NEXT.md.
#
# ## THE COLUMNS
#
# check       the name passed to ``--record``.
# command     exactly what to run. Copy it; do not paraphrase it.
# minutes     measured, not estimated. A number here comes from a run.
# due_after   commits on main since the last run before this is owed. The clock
#             is CHANGES, not days: a quiet fortnight owes nothing and five
#             sessions in a day owe the suite.
# artefact    the committed file this check writes, if it writes one. With it
#             filled in the last run is DERIVED -- ``git log -1 -- <artefact>``
#             -- and the three stamp columns are ignored and stay empty. That is
#             the whole reason the sweep needs no bookkeeping and the suite does.
# last_run    ISO date of the last ``--record``, for a row with no artefact.
# last_commit the short hash it was recorded at. ``never`` is DUE, not clean.
# last_result pass | fail. A FAILING check is recorded as failing. Stamping it
#             green to get a clean board is the one thing this file cannot
#             survive.
# note        what a failure was, in enough detail for the next session to
#             start from it rather than re-running to find out.
# on_surprise where the fix goes when the result is not what was expected. The
#             moment a 30-minute suite comes back red is the worst moment to be
#             working that out from scratch, so it is worked out in advance.
#
# ## WHY A LEDGER AND NOT A SCHEDULE
#
# A schedule would run these; this only says what is owed. The full suite is
# thirty minutes on the user's own machine and the standing rule is to ask
# first. What the rule was missing is a way to know how long it has been -- so
# a session can do a lot of work cheaply, and still hand over an honest account
# of what it did not run.
#
suite | python -m pytest -q | 30 | 4 |  | 2026-09-14 | e5dc47d | fail | T28. Run TWICE, either side of the fixes. Before: 11 failed / 1349 passed in 29m06s. After: 7 failed / 1353 passed in 29m48s. THE 7 ARE PRE-EXISTING AND NOT THIS SESSION'S -- measured by stashing the work and re-running the same 11 against a clean HEAD, where 7 already fail: test_fermentation x2, test_playable_levers, test_vanillin x2, test_vitriol x2. They are scoreboard pins that T3 and T2 moved without updating, and the last green suite (2026-09-13) predates both. The 4 that WERE mine were each a finding: test_critical x2 (a formation entry made HCN visible to a check that silently skips a species it cannot price -- 1.99% off 1 atm and 12.6% on Hvap, which moved the record out of the assembled tier into _CURATED_RAW with HF and water, residual named in BOILS_LOOSELY), test_protonation 42 -> 43 ions (cyanide, off a pKa row that had been unusable since it was written), test_playable RUNNABLE 49 -> 50 (andrussow, blocked on its own product). | the failing test names its own module; a green ./check.ps1 bounds 56 smoke tests and nothing else, so start from this session's diff, not from the test
tolerance | python validation/tolerance_audit.py | 10 | 6 |  | 2026-09-14 | e5dc47d | fail | T28. Owed because thermochemistry moved. NOTHING moved: same three findings and the same digits as 2026-09-13 and 2026-09-12 -- activity 0.1277%, multistep_prep 0.1073%, named_routes raises at rtol 1e-8. All still T10's pre-existing debt. Expected, and it is the point of running it: HCN and the nitrate esters appear in none of the twelve examples, so a curated entry and two Benson groups could not reach a trajectory, and now that is measured rather than argued. Tight is faster in 4 of 12 against last run's 3 of 12 -- timing noise on the same machine, not a finding. | a moved trajectory is numerics/vessel_integrator.py or the tolerances in numerics/integrator.py; a moved EQUILIBRIUM is thermochemistry, never the solver -- see rule 9
reachable | python tools/build_reachable.py | 35 | 10 | data/catalog/derived/reachable.psv |  |  |  |  | a changed reaction total is whichever of the four busiest templates moved; a changed templates_fired is the real finding and belongs in silent_templates.psv
routes | python examples/named_routes.py | 1 | 4 |  | 2026-09-14 | 5964f16 | pass | T2: 17 routes in 38.9 s, exit 0. The extractor's 54 rows are tier=literal and every engine path goes through ui.examples.full_library(), which defaults to tier=family -- so the library this example runs is the same 67 rows it ran on 2026-09-13 and nothing in the output moved. | a route that stops ending is its own step's template or a species that lost its price; run it with the route named before touching anything
playable | python tools/build_playable.py | 1 | 8 | data/catalog/PLAYABLE.md |  |  |  |  | regenerate, never edit; a moved tier count is a shelf row or a route_steps class, and check.ps1 -Full catches staleness already
